@page "/docs/troubleshooting/password-after-post"
@attribute [FollowsModeSwitch]

@* TextField doesn't echo a password into static HTML (c0070c8, EchoesValue in TextField.razor):
   after a failed static post the field is empty. By design; what to do instead. Checked in a
   scratch page and in text-field-sign-in in all three modes. *@

<ArticlePage Section="troubleshooting" Slug="password-after-post">
    <Lead>
        This is on purpose: a static page doesn't write a password back into its HTML. Ask for it again, and catch what you
        can before the form posts.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Problem</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            On a static page, a sign-in or sign-up form posts and the server turns it down. The page comes back with the
            other fields filled in and the error showing, but the password field is empty. On an interactive page the
            password stays.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Cause</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            After a static post, the page comes back as new HTML with each field's value in it. For a password that would
            send it back to the browser in the page itself, so, like most server-rendered forms, <Code>TextField</Code>
            leaves a password's <Code>value</Code> out on static pages. An interactive page updates the field in place,
            with no new HTML, so it keeps what was typed.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Fix</Heading>
        <TextBlock>There's nothing to turn off. Make the empty field easy to deal with:</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Say so.</strong> Put "Enter your password again" in the message, so the empty field isn't a
                surprise.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Check before posting.</strong> Keep <Code>NativeValidation</Code> on (the default): the browser checks
                <Code>[Required]</Code>, lengths and formats before the form posts, so those mistakes keep the password.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Set <Code>AutoComplete</Code>.</strong> <Code>current-password</Code> or <Code>new-password</Code>
                lets a password manager fill it again.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="SignIn.razor" HighlightLines="5,9">@Fixed</Code>
        <TextBlock>Sign in with any password: the server turns it down.</TextBlock>
        <ExampleViewer Example="text-field-sign-in" ModeSwitch />

        <Heading Level="2">Related</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/text-field">TextField</Anchor>: passwords and validation.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/forms">Forms and validation</Anchor>: checked before and after posting.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Fixed = """
        <Form Model="Model" FormName="sign-in" OnValidSubmit="SignInAsync">
            @if (_failed)
            {
                <Alert Status="Status.Danger">
                    That email and password don't match. Enter your password again.
                </Alert>
            }
            <TextField @bind-Value="Model!.Email" AutoComplete="username" />
            <TextField @bind-Value="Model!.Password" AutoComplete="current-password" />
            <Button Submit>Sign in</Button>
        </Form>
        """;
}
